      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "In the context of long-term systemic risk, many policy experts argue that the rising costs of climate change and infrastructure failure represent a greater, more certain economic burden than the initial friction of a well-designed fee. This view suggests that failing to implement necessary policies now will lead to significantly higher societal and economic costs later on. Therefore, some consider aggressive environmental action a necessary precondition for stable long-term economic health, placing necessity above immediate political convenience.",
      "source": "target",
